<h1>Endpoints Config Types</h1>
<p>Up to this point every step of the tutorial ran on the controller's own machine. Orcraft can send a step to
	any endpoint registered in the controller instead, and the test decides where its steps go through
	<strong>endpoint fields</strong> on its config class. An endpoint field is a configuration field like the folder
	and selector fields seen earlier, except that its value is a machine rather than a path or a number. The test
	body never names a machine: the flows hand each step the endpoint field to run on, and the value of that field
	is chosen in the run panel. The same suite therefore runs on the local machine while it is being written and
	across a lab once it is done, with no change to its code.</p>
<p>The framework offers a few <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/#declaring">field types</a>, and
	the difference between them is what happens when more than one machine is selected:</p>
<ul>
	<li><strong>Endpoint</strong> holds one machine. A test declares one such field per role it needs: a generator
		and a solver, a server and a client, a host and a device.</li>
	<li><strong>MultiSelectEndpoints</strong> holds a set of machines that take part together in one run. The
		test loops over them and decides what each one does.</li>
	<li><strong>EndpointFanout</strong> also holds a set of machines, but each machine gets a run of its own. The
		same test is repeated once per selected endpoint, and the runs execute in parallel.</li>
</ul>
<p>In the run panel every endpoint field shows its chosen machines and an edit button that opens a dialog listing
	the endpoints registered on <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/endpoints-management/">the previous
	page</a>. Two rows accompany the endpoint fields. <strong>Upload Test Package</strong> decides whether the
	suite's own code is sent to the endpoint before the run, and <strong>Update Endpoint App</strong> whether the
	endpoint application on the machine is brought to the controller's version first; both default to doing so only
	when needed, and both are explained on the next page. A package field such as
	<strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong> additionally shows an <strong>Endpoints</strong> line and an
	<strong>Upload</strong> choice, because its files travel to the machines where they are used.</p>
<p>The <strong>Endpoints</strong> group of the tutorial suite declares one test per field type. All of them reuse
	the flows and steps of <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/structure-and-lifecycle/">Structure and
	Lifecycle</a> and all need their <strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong> set, and this page follows them with the
	Windows, Linux and macOS machines registered earlier.</p>

<h2 id="a-single-endpoint">A single endpoint</h2>
<p>An <code>Endpoint</code> field holds one machine. Its value is one of the endpoints registered in the controller,
	and every step of the test that does not name another endpoint runs on that machine. It is the simplest way to
	move a test off the controller: nothing in the test changes, only the machine chosen in the panel. A test that
	declares a single endpoint field runs entirely on one machine, whichever it is.</p>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_default_endpoint_and_package_upload/") }}
<p>Every test config inherits a <code>test_endpoint</code> field that defaults to <code>localhost</code>. It stays
	hidden in the run panel until a test declares it, which <strong>Default Endpoint and Package Upload</strong>
	does with one line:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('test_endpoint = OrcraftConfigField.Endpoint(default="localhost", label="Test Endpoint")') }}
<p>A step that names no endpoint of its own runs on this one, so declaring the field is enough to move the whole
	test to another machine. Select the test and click the edit button at the right of
	<strong>Test Endpoint</strong>. Choose the Linux machine:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/single-endpoint-config.png", "Default Endpoint and Package Upload run panel with Test Endpoint set to GMG-TEST-3, Upload Test Package set to If Missing, Update Endpoint App set to On Mismatch, and the Maze Executable Folder showing Endpoints All and Upload If Missing", "large") }}
<p>Click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong>, then <strong class="text-purple">Run</strong> and
	confirm. Open the run's Details when it finishes:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/single-endpoint-details.png", "Default Endpoint and Package Upload Details view with every step row carrying GMG-TEST-3 in its Endpoints column and a passing status", "large") }}
<p>Every step row carries <strong>GMG-TEST-3</strong> in its <strong>Endpoints</strong> column. The steps ran on
	the Linux machine, with the Linux build of the Maze application that the package folder also holds, and their
	results came back to the controller exactly as they did from <strong>localhost</strong>.</p>

<h2 id="endpoints-with-roles">Endpoints with roles</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_multiple_endpoints_and_files_transfer/") }}
<p>Some scenarios need more than one machine at a time, each with its own job: something that produces and
	something that consumes, a server and a client, a host and the device attached to it. For that a test declares
	several <code>Endpoint</code> fields, one per role, and its flows send each step to the field of the role the step
	belongs to. The roles are fixed by the test; which machine plays which role is chosen in the panel, and the same
	machine may be chosen for more than one role.</p>
<p><strong>Multiple Endpoints and Files Transfer</strong> declares two such roles. It generates mazes on the
	first and solves them on the second, moving the files between them in the meantime:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('endpoint_1 = OrcraftConfigField.Endpoint(default="localhost", label="Endpoint 1")
endpoint_2 = OrcraftConfigField.Endpoint(default="localhost", label="Endpoint 2")') }}
<p>The flows pass an <code>endpoint</code> argument to <code>run_step</code> so that each step goes to the machine
	of its role, and the <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/#transfer">file transfer</a> between the
	two is a framework call the flow makes between the steps. Set <strong>Endpoint 1</strong> to the Windows
	machine and <strong>Endpoint 2</strong> to the macOS machine, update the plan and run. The Details show the
	split:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/two-endpoints-details.png", "Multiple Endpoints and Files Transfer Details view with the Delete and Generate steps of every flow marked GMG-TEST-1 and the Load / Solve steps marked GMG-TEST-5", "large") }}
<p>Within each flow the <strong>Delete Existing Maze File</strong> and <strong>Generate / Save</strong> steps ran
	on <strong>GMG-TEST-1</strong> and the <strong>Load / Solve</strong> steps on <strong>GMG-TEST-5</strong>. The
	configuration table on the left records the plan and run values of both fields with their ports, so a run can
	always be traced back to the machines it used.</p>

<h2 id="a-set-of-endpoints">A set of endpoints</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_multi_select_endpoints/") }}
<p>A <code>MultiSelectEndpoints</code> field holds a set of machines rather than one. The set is still one role in
	one run: the test receives all the selected endpoints together, loops over them, and decides what each one does.
	This is the type for a role whose size is not fixed, such as any number of devices that one host drives, or any
	number of solvers fed by one generator. Selecting three machines does not repeat the test; it gives the single run
	three machines to work with.</p>
<p><strong>Multi-Select Endpoints</strong> keeps one generator and takes any number of solvers:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('test_endpoint = OrcraftConfigField.Endpoint(default="localhost", label="Test Endpoint")
aux_endpoints = OrcraftConfigField.MultiSelectEndpoints(default=("localhost",), label="Aux Endpoints")') }}
<p>A <code>MultiSelectEndpoints</code> field holds a tuple of endpoints, and the test body loops over it, running
	the three transfer flows against each solver in turn. Choose the Windows machine as the
	<strong>Test Endpoint</strong> and the Linux and macOS machines as <strong>Aux Endpoints</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/multi-select-config.png", "Multi-Select Endpoints run panel with Test Endpoint set to GMG-TEST-1 and the Aux Endpoints multi-select field listing GMG-TEST-3 and GMG-TEST-5", "large") }}
<p>The field lists its selected endpoints one per line. It is still one run: the four cases each visit both
	solvers, and the endpoints play parts in one scenario rather than repeating it.</p>

<h2 id="fan-out">Fan-out</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_endpoint_fanout/") }}
<p>An <code>EndpointFanout</code> field also holds a set of machines, but with the opposite meaning: the test is
	repeated once per selected endpoint, and each repetition is a run of its own. The runs start together and execute
	in parallel, each appears as its own <strong>Test Run</strong> row in the execution tree with the machine it ran on,
	and the test's plan is the case count multiplied by the number of machines. This is the type for running the
	same verification on every machine of a lab, for example the same application check on Windows, Linux and
	macOS.</p>
<p><strong>Endpoint Fanout</strong> runs the flows of the first test, but redeclares the inherited field:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('test_endpoint = OrcraftConfigField.EndpointFanout(default=("localhost",), label="Test Endpoint")') }}
<p>An <code>EndpointFanout</code> field also holds a tuple, but each selected endpoint gets a run of its own.
	Select all three machines:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/fanout-config.png", "Endpoint Fanout run panel with the Test Endpoint (Fanout) field listing GMG-TEST-1, GMG-TEST-3 and GMG-TEST-5 and the counter reporting Total Cases 12", "large") }}
<p><strong>Total Cases</strong> reads 12 for a matrix of four cases: the four cases three times over, once per
	machine. Update the plan and run:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/fanout-runs-tree.png", "Execution tree with the Endpoint Fanout test planning 12 cases and holding three passing Test Run rows, whose Endpoints column reads GMG-TEST-1, GMG-TEST-3 and GMG-TEST-5 with a fan-out mark", "large") }}
<p>The test row plans 12 and holds three <strong>Test Run</strong> rows that ran at the same time. Each row's
	<strong>Endpoints</strong> column names its machine with a fan-out mark, and each has its own
	<strong class="status-pass">Pass</strong> in the <strong>Status</strong> column. Fan-out is the way to run
	one verification everywhere; the multi-select field is the way to let several machines take part in one
	run.</p>

<h3 id="queued-runs">Queued runs</h3>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_endpoint_fanout_queued/") }}
<p>A run holds the endpoints it uses for as long as it runs, because a test usually owns the machine it is
	working on: two runs writing to the same files or driving the same device at once would corrupt each other's
	results. When a second run needs an endpoint that a first run is holding, the second run is queued and starts
	only when the endpoint is released. Queueing is automatic; the tree shows it as a status.</p>
<p>This matters when a fan-out and a fixed endpoint meet in one test. In
	<strong>Endpoint Fanout - Queued</strong> the generator, <strong>Endpoint 1</strong>, is a plain endpoint,
	and the solver, <strong>Endpoint 2</strong>, is a fan-out, so every fanned-out run needs the same generator.
	Choose the Windows machine as the generator and the Linux and macOS machines as solvers, then run:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/fanout-queued-tree.png", "Execution tree during the Endpoint Fanout - Queued run, with the first Test Run row running on GMG-TEST-1 and GMG-TEST-3 and the second row, for GMG-TEST-1 and GMG-TEST-5, showing Queued in its Status column", "large") }}
<p>Two runs are created, one per solver, but they do not start together. Both need the generator, so the second
	row shows <strong>Queued</strong> in its <strong>Status</strong> column until the first run releases
	<strong>GMG-TEST-1</strong>, then runs in turn. Machines that can serve several runs at once are marked as
	shared on their field, which lifts the queueing; the framework reference describes the option under
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/#endpoint-options">field options</a>.</p>

<h3 id="permutations">Permutations</h3>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_endpoint_fanout_permutations/") }}
<p>A test can declare more than one fan-out field. The runs are then the combinations, or permutations, of the
	selections: every machine chosen for the first role paired with every machine chosen for the second. Two
	machines in each of two roles give four runs, three in each give nine. Because that grows quickly, the panel
	lists the combinations and lets you keep only the ones worth running.</p>
<p><strong>Endpoint Fanout - Permutations</strong> declares both its roles as fan-outs. Select the Windows and
	the Linux machine for both <strong>Endpoint 1</strong> and <strong>Endpoint 2</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/fanout-permutations-config.png", "Endpoint Fanout - Permutations run panel with GMG-TEST-1 and GMG-TEST-3 selected in both fan-out fields and a Fanout Endpoints Permutations table listing the four combinations, all ticked, with Total Cases 16", "large") }}
<p>A <strong>Fanout Endpoints Permutations</strong> table appears with one row per combination, four here, each
	with a check mark in <strong>Select</strong>. Clearing a row drops that combination from the plan, which is how
	a machine can be excluded from solving its own mazes without touching either field. With all four selected, the
	four cases run 16 times. Update the plan and run:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/fanout-permutations-tree.png", "Execution tree after the Endpoint Fanout - Permutations run, with the test planning 16 cases and four passing Test Run rows whose Endpoints column shows each combination of GMG-TEST-1 and GMG-TEST-3", "large") }}
<p>Four <strong>Test Run</strong> rows, one per combination, each naming its generator and solver in the
	<strong>Endpoints</strong> column, and the test row sums them to 16 planned and 16 passed.</p>

<p>The next page, <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/packages-uploading/">Packages Uploading</a>, follows the
	package fields that carried the Maze application to these machines, and shows how a field is tied to the
	endpoints that need its files.</p>
